WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.513
Last video for week two.

2
00:00:01.563 --> 00:00:04.954
We have the loop, the harness, tools and MCP.

3
00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:08.395
The question now is how much the model gets to decide.

4
00:00:08.445 --> 00:00:16.840
We treat that as a dial, not a switch, and the position of the dial is a design choice your team will write down in the proposal.

5
00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:18.902
Here is the dial.

6
00:00:18.952 --> 00:00:26.049
At the left, the model suggests and a person acts: think of a draft reply the person must copy and send.

7
00:00:26.099 --> 00:00:31.495
One step right, the model drafts and the person approves every output before it goes anywhere.

8
00:00:31.545 --> 00:00:41.025
In the middle, the model acts on its own for routine steps, and a gate stops it before anything risky or irreversible, where a person must say yes.

9
00:00:41.075 --> 00:00:46.811
Further right, the model acts and reports, and a person reviews afterwards, by sampling or by exception.

10
00:00:46.861 --> 00:00:51.917
At the far right, the agent runs unattended and nobody looks unless an alert fires.

11
00:00:51.967 --> 00:00:56.341
The same model and the same tools can sit anywhere on this dial.

12
00:00:56.391 --> 00:01:00.766
What moves it is harness design: who acts, and when a person looks.

13
00:01:01.516 --> 00:01:03.570
Recall the distinction from week one.

14
00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:08.128
A workflow fixes the steps in code and the model fills each step.

15
00:01:08.178 --> 00:01:15.141
Its cost and behavior are predictable and it is easy to test, because the same input follows the same path.

16
00:01:15.191 --> 00:01:17.946
An agent lets the model choose the steps.

17
00:01:17.996 --> 00:01:26.011
It handles cases nobody wrote down, and it is harder to test, because the same input can take different paths on different runs.

18
00:01:26.061 --> 00:01:30.569
Workflows naturally sit toward the left of the dial, agents toward the right.

19
00:01:30.619 --> 00:01:37.231
Most good systems are a workflow with an agent inside it, at the one step where judgment is needed.

20
00:01:37.981 --> 00:01:42.943
An approval gate is a specific piece of harness code, so let us be concrete.

21
00:01:42.993 --> 00:01:48.957
Before a named tool call, say send email or issue refund, the loop pauses and saves its state.

22
00:01:49.007 --> 00:01:54.971
A person sees the proposed action with its arguments and the reasons, and approves, edits or rejects it.

23
00:01:55.021 --> 00:01:58.312
The loop resumes from the saved state carrying the decision.

24
00:01:58.362 --> 00:02:02.990
And the pause itself goes into the trace, with who approved what and when.

25
00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.997
In LangGraph this is an interrupt on a node.

26
00:02:06.047 --> 00:02:09.339
In Fleet it is an approval setting on a tool.

27
00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:17.024
In both cases the important part is that the gate lives in the harness, where the model cannot talk its way past it.

28
00:02:17.774 --> 00:02:18.979
How do you choose?

29
00:02:19.029 --> 00:02:29.966
Move left when a mistake is costly or irreversible, when you have no evidence yet of how often the agent fails, and when the user is not the person who gets hurt by a mistake.

30
00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:40.325
Move right when errors are cheap and visible, when you have measured the failure rate and can live with it, and when reviewing the agent's work is genuinely faster than doing the task.

31
00:02:40.375 --> 00:02:44.092
Notice that two of the three conditions on the right require measurement.

32
00:02:44.142 --> 00:02:45.976
That is why week four exists.

33
00:02:46.026 --> 00:02:49.115
You cannot earn a position on the dial without numbers.

34
00:02:49.865 --> 00:02:57.431
In the proposal your team submits after this week, state the dial position for each tool rather than for the agent as a whole.

35
00:02:57.481 --> 00:03:01.239
Reading the calendar can be unattended while sending the invitation is gated.

36
00:03:01.289 --> 00:03:08.537
Name the gate: which action pauses, who approves, and how quickly they must answer, because a gate nobody answers is a stuck service.

37
00:03:08.587 --> 00:03:12.663
And say what evidence would let you move one step to the right.

38
00:03:12.713 --> 00:03:16.153
That sentence is the bridge to the rest of the course.

39
00:03:16.203 --> 00:03:21.548
Next week: context, which is what the model sees when it decides, and how to control it.
